  "headline": "Dollar at a high, gold at a low: the market is pricing a Fed that will not blink",
  "body": [
    "Gold closed at a new yearly low of $3,888 while the dollar index made a new yearly high at 103.4, and that pair is the whole story. Yields rose again with the front end leading, the 5-year up 33 basis points on the week against 22 on the 30-year, so this is a market pricing a Fed that holds or hikes into an oil shock, not one financing the Treasury. Drop Site reports Iran hit a US radar in Bahrain, and Brent managed 0.35%.",
    "Yesterday I said the thesis was broken on gold and the dollar and that I would take the market's side; today only widened the break. My trigger for covering the rupee short was Brent holding $95 while the rupee stopped firming. Brent sits at $96 and USD/INR rose 0.6% today, so I cover.",
    "Everything except the dollar is being sold, bonds included, yet stocks are up 2.5% on the week against a 10-year up 30 basis points. That is growth plus tighter policy, not the fiscal squeeze I look for, where equities fall as the long end cheapens. Bitcoin is down 11% against gold on the month: liquidity being pulled from the margins, not fear of the long bond. The VIX at 19, in the bottom third of its range, says gold's slide is orderly selling under rising real rates, and orderly is worse for me because nobody has to capitulate back.",
    "An Iranian strike on a US radar in Bahrain, as Drop Site describes it, is exactly the event my energy leg needs, and oil shrugged: Brent at $96 is still down 9% on the month. The yen barely moved and the loonie cannot catch a bid from crude near $96, so the market is saying supply is fine and the war's cost is being paid in the policy rate, not at the pump. Until Brent takes $100 the market has priced this better than I have.",
    "My book: out of gold, and the way back in is unchanged, a close above $4,000 on a day the dollar falls. Out of the 30-year at 5.48%, because a believed Fed lands the pain on the front end first. I keep Brent as the one war hedge with a chance of paying, cover USD/INR, and will not fight the dollar at a yearly high. What proves me wrong on the bear side is gold breaking $3,885 on a day the dollar also falls, which would be forced selling rather than real rates. Next I watch the 30-year minus 5-year spread: through 60 basis points with stocks lower, and the old view is back."
  ],
